{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgdYyA57QT2JZtD7uuKrZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"NVIDIA has released Nemotron 3.5 Transcribe, a speech model that takes a text instruction along with the audio. One checkpoint can transcribe a meeting with speaker labels, translate it into English, or answer a question about it. Language support differs by task, audio question answering is trained in English, and the model is built for recorded audio rather than live streaming.\n\nThe benchmark results are strong, though they come from different sources. NVIDIA reports 5.33% average word error rate on the English track of the Hugging Face Open ASR Leaderboard, first among open-weight models. Artificial Analysis, which ranks models independently, has it tied for first among open-weight models at 2.8% AA-WER. It reaches that with about 4B active parameters, against 24B for the model it ties. Speaker cues from a Sortformer component reach the language model alongside the acoustic features, so the model tags turns while it generates the transcript. On David AI's 21-language diarization track it has the lowest error rate of 14 systems, 18.26%, just ahead of NVIDIA's dedicated diarization model at 18.38%.\n\nThe most practical part for anyone with specialized audio is the adaptation case study. It uses Eka Care's benchmark of recordings from Indian doctors and medical students. Naming the language and script in the prompt cut full-benchmark WER from 30.8% to 23.8%. Fine-tuning on synthetic speech, accented English and real medical recordings took about 14 GPU-hours on one GB300. On 2,764 held-out clips from speakers excluded from tuning, it cut WER from 22.44% to 12.17%, a 45.8% relative reduction. That was ahead of an open medical-specialist model at 13.79% and a general-purpose ASR model at 14.92%.\n\nThe study also reports costs. LibriSpeech test-clean WER rose from 1.54% to 1.69%, and the medical specialist still leads on exact matches for brand-name drugs and diagnostics. The 855 tuning clips were used to choose the prompt and checkpoints, which is why the held-out numbers are the ones to trust. For a similar project, the authors suggest keeping at least 100 real, labeled clips for checkpoint selection and scoring the entity types the application depends on, not only overall WER."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6676,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6676},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"36151e93-a157-48a1-8c2d-a29815113b4f","uuid":"6a94f1ae-5df3-4ce7-8fd1-354e46a746a9","timestamp":"2026-11-02T05:30:41.806Z","request_id":"req_011CgdYy9sSrsHsEx44G3zb8"}
